Fix $n\ge2$. For every sufficiently large integer $N$ one can choose a system of homogeneous polynomials of degree $N$, $$Q_N=(H_N,p_N):\mathbb C^n\longrightarrow\mathbb C^{n-1}\times\mathbb C,$$ with the following properties. These choices can be made along all integers $N\to\infty$, or along any prescribed sequence of degrees tending to infinity.*


1.  *There is a constant $C_n$ such that $$\label{grid:sphere-bounds}
     N^{-C_n}\le |Q_N(u)|\le N\qquad (|u|=1).$$*


2.  *The projective zero set of $H_N$ consists of $M_N=N^{n-1}$ transverse points. A subset $\mathcal G_N$ of these points satisfies $$|\mathcal G_N|=(1-o(1))M_N,
     \qquad
     \frac1{M_N}\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal G_N}\delta_\xi
     \ \rightharpoonup\ \sigma.$$ For every $\xi\in\mathcal G_N$ and every unit representative $u$ of $\xi$, $$\label{grid:goodness}
     N^{-1}\le |p_N(u)|\le N,
     \qquad
     \operatorname{sing}_{\min}
           \bigl(DH_N(u)|_{u^\perp}\bigr)\ge N^{-1}.$$*


3.  *Above each $\xi\in\mathcal G_N$, choose any lift $v_\xi$ such that $p_N(v_\xi)=1$. All $N$ lifts $$e^{2\pi i q/N}v_\xi,\qquad 0\le q<N,$$ have radii in $[N^{-1/N},N^{1/N}]$. With the absolute choice $k_*=30$, the Euclidean balls of radius $2N^{-k_*}$ about all these lifts are pairwise disjoint. If $v$ is one of the lifts, then $$\label{grid:shell-bound}
     |(H_N(z),p_N(z)-1)|\ge N^{-k_*-10}
     \quad\hbox{when}\quad
     N^{-k_*}\le |z-v|\le2N^{-k_*}.$$ In a neighborhood of radius $4N^{-k_*}$ of every such lift, the first and second derivatives of $Q_N$ have norm $O_n(N^5)$.*

*In particular, weak approximation to any prescribed finite collection of continuous test functions, with any fixed positive accuracy, can be required by taking $N$ sufficiently large.

Fix positive integers $k,n$, with $n\ge2$, such that $$\label{grid:dimension-choice}
 \sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\frac{j^{n-1}}{(n-1)!}<\frac1{100}.$$ Fix a nonnegative integer $h$. For sufficiently large polynomial grids from Lemma <a href="#grid:polynomials" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="grid:polynomials">4</a>, choose one lift $v_\xi$ above each good direction and let $V_N\subset\mathbb C^{\mathcal G_N}$ be the space of evaluation vectors of sums of homogeneous polynomials of degrees $$h,\ h+N,\ \ldots,\ h+(k-1)N.$$ Let $\Pi_N$ be orthogonal projection onto $V_N$ for the unweighted Euclidean point norm. There are phases $b_\xi$, $|b_\xi|=1$, and a constant $\delta_h>0$, independent of $N$ and of the chosen lift phases, such that $$\label{grid:phase-lower-bound}
 M_N^{-1/2}
 \left\|(1-\Pi_N)
       \bigl(b_\xi P(v_\xi)\bigr)_{\xi\in\mathcal G_N}\right\|
 \ge\delta_h\|P\|$$ for every homogeneous polynomial $P$ of degree $h$. Here $\|P\|$ is any fixed Euclidean norm on its coefficient space, chosen independently of the grid. All required grid accuracies and degree thresholds depend only on the fixed $n,k,h$ and this norm.

Let $0\le h<N$, and suppose $g$ is holomorphic on a neighborhood of the closed ball $|s|\le S$, with $|g|\le K$ there. Write $g=\sum_{r\ge0}g_r$ for its homogeneous Taylor expansion at zero. If $R|v|<S$, then $$\frac1N\sum_{q=0}^{N-1}e^{-2\pi i hq/N}
       g(Re^{2\pi i q/N}v)
   =\sum_{j\ge0}g_{h+jN}(Rv).$$ If $R|v|/S\le e^{-0.99c/N}$ and $c\ge1$, truncating the sum after $j=k-1$ has error at most $C K e^{-0.99kc}$, while retaining just the term $g_h(Rv)$ has error at most $C K e^{-0.99c}$. The constant $C$ is absolute, independent of $N,h,R,S$.

In tilted logarithmic coordinates, the tensor <a href="#fiber:curvature-convention" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fiber:curvature-convention">[fiber:curvature-convention]</a> has the following blocks: $$\begin{aligned}
 A:=C_{0\bar0 0\bar0}
   &=P^2P_{\tau\tau}-|\partial P|_h^2,
 \label{fiber:blockA}\\
 A_i:=C_{i\bar0 0\bar0}
   &=P\bigl(\partial_iP_\tau-
       (\mathcal N h^{-1}\partial P)_i\bigr),
 \label{fiber:blockAi}\\
 W_{i\bar j}:=C_{i\bar j0\bar0}
   &=P\bigl(\partial_i\partial_{\bar j}\log P
       +P_\tau\mathcal N_{i\bar j}
       -P(\mathcal N h^{-1}\mathcal N)_{i\bar j}\bigr),
 \label{fiber:blockW}\\
 C_{i\bar0 k\bar0}&=(\nabla_h^{2,0}P)_{ik},
 \label{fiber:blockspin}\\
 K_{ik\bar j}:=C_{i\bar j k\bar0}
   &=P\nabla_i^h\mathcal N_{k\bar j}
      +P_i\mathcal N_{k\bar j}+P_k\mathcal N_{i\bar j},
 \label{fiber:blockK}\\
 L_{i\bar j k\bar l}:=C_{i\bar j k\bar l}
   &=C^h_{i\bar j k\bar l}
      +P(\mathcal N_{i\bar j}\mathcal N_{k\bar l}
          +\mathcal N_{i\bar l}\mathcal N_{k\bar j}).
 \label{fiber:blockL}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $C^h$ and $\nabla^h$ are the curvature with sign <a href="#fiber:curvature-convention" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fiber:curvature-convention">[fiber:curvature-convention]</a> and the Kähler connection of the fixed-$\tau$ spatial metric. Index positions in the products use the indicated Hermitian metric, with the usual conjugations.

If $C(H,H)\ge0$ for every Hermitian contravariant matrix $H$ of rank at most two, the underlying real sectional curvatures are nonpositive. Write the spatial, cross, and fiber diagonal blocks of $H$ as $T,b_\times,t$. The contraction has the terms $$\label{fiber:contraction}
 At^2+2t(W:T)+2W(b_\times,\bar b_\times)+L(T,T),$$ and its other terms have absolute value at most $$\label{fiber:otherterms}
 2|\nabla_h^{2,0}P(b_\times,b_\times)|
 +4|tA_i b_\times^i|+4|(K:T)b_\times|.$$ In particular the following quantitative criterion suffices. Suppose $A>0$, $W>0$, and a nonnegative quadratic form $\mathcal R(T)$ satisfies $$\begin{aligned}
 L(T,T)-\frac{|W:T|^2}{.98A}&\ge\mathcal R(T),
 \label{fiber:reserve}\\
 |\nabla_h^{2,0}P(b,b)|&\le\tfrac14 W(b,\bar b),
 \label{fiber:spinabs}\\
 |A_i b^i|&\le\eta\sqrt{A W(b,\bar b)},
 \label{fiber:Aiabs}\\
 |(K:T)b|&\le\eta\sqrt{\mathcal R(T)W(b,\bar b)},
 \label{fiber:Kabs}
\end{aligned}$$ for all such tests, where $\eta\le1/100$. Then $$\label{fiber:retained}
 C(H,H)\ge .01At^2+W(b_\times,\bar b_\times)
                         +\tfrac12\mathcal R(T).$$ All constants are independent of dimension.

In any fixed holomorphic coordinates let $H$ denote the cubic jet viewed as a map from two tangent inputs to a covector, and write $H=\Gamma g$. For a perturbation with $g+\Delta g>0$, the change of the cubic contraction $Hg^{-1}H^*$ is exactly $$\label{fiber:perturbformula}
 \Gamma\Delta H^*+\Delta H\Gamma^*-\Gamma\Delta g\Gamma^*
 +(\Delta H-\Gamma\Delta g)(g+\Delta g)^{-1}
                 (\Delta H-\Gamma\Delta g)^*.$$ Consequently, if $\|g^{-1}\Delta g\|$ in metric-unit axes is at most one half, the inverse of the perturbed metric is at most twice that of $g$, and $$\begin{aligned}
\label{fiber:perturbbound}
 \|\Delta C\|\le {}&\|\Delta j^4\ell\|
       +2\|\Gamma\|\|\Delta H\|
       +\|\Gamma\|^2\|\Delta g\|\\
 &+2\|g^{-1}\|
       (\|\Delta H\|+\|\Gamma\|\|\Delta g\|)^2.
 \notag
\end{aligned}$$ The same bound, with an absolute factor, controls rank-two contractions.

Suppose $a,a_\psi,Y>0$, $\lambda\ge1$, and $$\label{radial:kappa-assumptions}
 \frac1{1+Y}\le\kappa\le1.1,\qquad
 \frac{d\kappa}{d\log Y}\ge-\kappa.$$ Then $$\begin{aligned}
\label{radial:clock-equations}
 \frac{d\nu}{du}&=\frac1Y,&
 \frac{d\log Y}{du}&=\kappa(1+1/Y),&
 \frac{d\log\lambda}{du}&=1-p,\\
 \frac{d\Psi}{du}&=\frac1H,&
 \frac{d\log H}{du}&=\kappa(1+1/Y)-p-1/Y.\notag
\end{aligned}$$ If $Y\ge t$ initially, then $Y\ge t$ subsequently. With smooth bounded controls $p,\kappa$ on each finite $u$ interval, positive solutions have no blowup on that interval. A cap of the form $\kappa=C_\kappa/(1+Y)$, with fixed $C_\kappa\ge1$, has no blowup at finite $\nu$ for the prescribed bounded controls.*


*If $p=1$, then $$\label{radial:H-logY}
 \frac{d\log H}{d\log Y}=1-\frac1\kappa.$$ In particular, $p=\kappa=1$ holds both $\lambda$ and $H$ constant. If $\kappa\ge1$ on an interval beginning at $u_0$, then the total increase of $\nu$ on that interval is at most $1/Y(u_0)$, even if its finite $u$ length is arbitrarily large.

Under <a href="#radial:kappa-assumptions" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="radial:kappa-assumptions">[radial:kappa-assumptions]</a>, the negative Hermitian curvature tensor $C^V=-R^V$ satisfies $$\label{radial:rank-reserve}
 C^V(T,T)\ge c\,\kappa\frac{H}{\lambda}|T|_V^2
 \qquad\text{if }T=T^*,\quad\operatorname{rank}T\le2,$$ where $c>0$ is absolute. Here the subscript $V$ means that the matrix is expressed in $V$-unit axes.

There is an absolute exponent $C_{\rm pol}$ such that, for $t\geq1$ and $1\leq r\leq4$, $$\label{assembly:well-raw}
 |\partial^{[r]}W_j|
 \leq C(n,d)\exp\{C_{\rm pol}(\max(l,0)+c+\log N)\}.$$ There is a constant $L_n<\infty$ such that, after increasing the degree threshold, $$\label{assembly:well-tail}
 |\partial^{[r]}W_j|\leq N^{L_n}
 \qquad(1\leq r\leq4,\ l\geq L_n\log N).$$ Both statements hold throughout the indicated outer ranges.
